WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.578
You want one function that returns the bigger of two values.

2
00:00:06.578 --> 00:00:08.776
Amounts, currency codes, anything.

3
00:00:08.776 --> 00:00:11.168
So you write it once, for any type T.

4
00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:14.662
In Kotlin, a &gt; b means a.compareTo(b).

5
00:00:14.662 --> 00:00:16.223
Does this compile?

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.223
No.

7
00:00:18.223 --> 00:00:22.315
T has no bound, so the caller may pick any type at all.

8
00:00:22.315 --> 00:00:24.399
An Int, a String, a Payment.

9
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.460
Inside, your code may only use what all of them share.

10
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.248
Kotlin's default bound is Any?, and Java's is Object.

11
00:00:32.248 --> 00:00:35.393
Both give you toString, hashCode and equals.

12
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.682
And a Payment has no order.

13
00:00:37.682 --> 00:00:41.307
Should it compare by amount, by time, or by id?

14
00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.620
So the compiler refuses a &gt; b.

15
00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:46.119
Java says it can't find compareTo.

16
00:00:46.119 --> 00:00:49.884
Kotlin finds only String's compareTo, which doesn't fit.

17
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:52.557
The fix is to promise more about T.

18
00:00:52.557 --> 00:00:54.265
Write T : Comparable&lt;T&gt;.

19
00:00:54.265 --> 00:00:58.748
Read it as: T is something that can be compared with another T.

20
00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:01.502
Now the bound is a gate at the call site.

21
00:01:01.502 --> 00:01:04.384
Int and String are comparable, so they get in.

22
00:01:04.384 --> 00:01:06.953
A call with a Payment no longer compiles.

23
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.490
Inside, the body gains a tool.

24
00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:13.516
The bound guarantees a compareTo, so a &gt; b compiles.

25
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:14.790
That is the trade.

26
00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:19.452
A bound lets fewer types in, and lets your code do more with the one it gets.

27
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.084
Now a function needs two abilities.

28
00:01:22.084 --> 00:01:26.716
shortestLabel finds the shortest label, and breaks ties alphabetically.

29
00:01:26.716 --> 00:01:28.673
It needs length and compareTo.

30
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:31.212
Angle brackets hold only one bound.

31
00:01:31.212 --> 00:01:35.561
The second goes in a where clause, and T must pass both at once.

32
00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:36.512
A String does.

33
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.480
An Int is comparable, but it has no characters, so it stops at the second gate.

34
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.113
Payment stops at the first.

35
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:44.961
What about a StringBuilder?

36
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:47.087
It gets in.

37
00:01:47.087 --> 00:01:50.657
Since JDK 11, StringBuilder is comparable too.

38
00:01:50.657 --> 00:01:53.528
Now the body may call length as well.

39
00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:00.757
Java writes a bound with extends, even for an interface, and joins two bounds with an ampersand.

40
00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:06.627
At most one bound may be a class, because a JVM class has only one superclass.

41
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.097
The rule.

42
00:02:08.097 --> 00:02:13.935
A bound shuts some callers out, and in return your code may use what the bound promises.

43
00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:14.972
Quick check.

44
00:02:14.972 --> 00:02:16.999
lengthOf only reads x.length.

45
00:02:16.999 --> 00:02:21.193
Which bound makes it compile, and can you still pass an Int?

46
00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:24.248
T : CharSequence.

47
00:02:24.248 --> 00:02:28.847
And no: an Int has no characters, so that call won't compile.
